# Tutorial 09 — Puppeteer Timeline
# Timeline blocks: parallel move tracks, event cues, camera tracks, and the visual editor.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_09_puppeteer
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: [move] handles one character at a time. When you need multiple actors, camera motion, and sound effects running simultaneously — you need a {b}timeline{/b}.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Timelines are VNS's most powerful feature. Let's learn the syntax.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
> Timeline block syntax -> section_what
> Move tracks — character animation -> section_move_tracks
> Event cues — timed triggers -> section_events
> Camera tracks — pan and zoom -> section_camera
> Keyframe paths — multi-point animation -> section_keyframes
> The Puppeteer visual editor -> section_editor
> Quick reference: timeline block -> quick_reference
> Practice: reading a timeline -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Inline timelines group animation instructions into one timed block:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}timeline \{{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  actor lavender x 640 at 0{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  actor lavender x 760 at 600 easeInOut{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  camera zoom 1.0 1.08 at 300 dur 500{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}\}{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Checklist: keep entity names stable, use keyframes for motion, and reserve timeline blocks for animation that needs precise timing.
> Start the lesson sections -> section_what
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Timeline Block ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_what
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: A {b}timeline{/b} block runs multiple tracks in parallel on one shared clock:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}timeline {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  move "lavender" {{}} from left to center easing easeInOut duration 600 {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  move "alex" {{}} from right to center easing easeOut duration 500 {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}{{}}{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Both characters move at the same time. No [wait] chains needed — the timeline synchronizes everything.
Lavender: The block contains {b}tracks{/b}. Each track is one animation channel: a move, an event, or a camera action.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The timeline ends when its longest track finishes. Script execution resumes after.
> Next: move tracks -> section_move_tracks
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Move Tracks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_move_tracks
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}Move tracks{/b} animate a character from one position to another:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}move "lavender" {{}} from far_left to center easing easeInOut duration 600 {{}}{/color}
narrator: Structure: move {i}"characterId"{/i} {{}} from {i}startPos{/i} to {i}endPos{/i} easing {i}curve{/i} duration {i}ms{/i} {{}}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Parameters:
narrator: {b}from{/b} — starting position (far_left, left, center, right, far_right, or at X,Y).
narrator: {b}to{/b} — ending position.
narrator: {b}easing{/b} — linear, easeIn, easeOut, easeInOut.
narrator: {b}duration{/b} — milliseconds.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Multiple move tracks for different characters run simultaneously inside the same timeline block.
> Next: event cues -> section_events
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Event Cues ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_events
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}Event tracks{/b} trigger instant actions at specific timestamps during the timeline:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}event "sfx" {{}} at 200ms: "door_slam.wav" {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}event "expression" {{}} at 400ms: "lavender" "surprised" {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}event "screen_shake" {{}} at 100ms: 0.3 150 {{}}{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Events don't animate over time — they fire at one exact moment.
Lavender: Use them to synchronize:
narrator: {b}Sound effects{/b} — a crash at the exact moment of impact.
narrator: {b}Expression changes{/b} — a character reacts at a precise time.
narrator: {b}Screen effects{/b} — shake or flash timed to movement.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The power: a character's expression changes at 400ms, exactly when they reach center position. Perfect sync.
> Next: camera tracks -> section_camera
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Camera Tracks ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_camera
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}Camera tracks{/b} animate the viewport — panning and zooming during the timeline:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}camera {{}} pan from 0,0 to 100,0 easing easeInOut duration 800 {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}camera {{}} zoom from 1.0 to 1.3 easing easeIn duration 600 {{}}{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Combine camera and character movement for cinematic shots:
narrator: {b}Tracking shot{/b} — camera pans as a character walks across the room.
narrator: {b}Dramatic zoom{/b} — camera pushes in during an emotional line.
narrator: {b}Establishing shot{/b} — slow pan across a new location.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Subtle camera work adds polish. Even a 5% zoom (1.0 → 1.05) during dialogue creates focus.
> Next: keyframe paths -> section_keyframes
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Keyframe Paths ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_keyframes
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: For complex motion paths, use {b}keyframes{/b} instead of simple from/to:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}move "lavender" {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  0ms: at 0.1,0.8{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  300ms: at 0.5,0.6{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  600ms: at 0.9,0.8{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}{{}}{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Each line is a timestamp and a position. The engine interpolates between them.
Lavender: The example above creates an arc — the character rises to center-top, then descends to the right.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Use from/to for straight-line A→B moves. Use keyframes for arcs, zigzags, and multi-stop paths.
> Next: the visual editor -> section_editor
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Puppeteer Editor ────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_editor
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: You don't have to write timelines by hand. The {b}Puppeteer{/b} is a visual timeline editor in the JVN Editor.
narrator: {b}1.{/b} Open the Puppeteer from the editor toolbar.
narrator: {b}2.{/b} Add characters to the stage.
narrator: {b}3.{/b} Drag keyframes on the timeline to set positions at specific moments.
narrator: {b}4.{/b} Add event cues and camera tracks visually.
narrator: {b}5.{/b} Preview the animation in real time.
narrator: {b}6.{/b} Export — the editor generates the VNS timeline block.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: You can also {b}import{/b} existing VNS timeline blocks back into the Puppeteer, edit visually, and re-export.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Visual for building, code for fine-tuning. The round-trip workflow means you're never locked into one approach.
> Practice: reading a timeline -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Let's read a complete timeline block together. I'll annotate each track.
narrator: Here's a full "two characters meet" timeline:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}timeline {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  move "lavender" {{}} from far_left to center easing easeInOut duration 600 {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  move "alex" {{}} from far_right to center easing easeOut duration 500 {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  event "sfx" {{}} at 0ms: "footsteps.wav" {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  event "expression" {{}} at 500ms: "lavender" "happy" {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}  camera {{}} zoom from 1.0 to 1.1 easing easeIn duration 600 {{}}{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}{{}}{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Breakdown:
narrator: {b}Track 1:{/b} Lavender moves from far_left to center over 600ms with easeInOut.
narrator: {b}Track 2:{/b} Alex moves from far_right to center over 500ms with easeOut (arrives slightly early).
narrator: {b}Track 3:{/b} A footstep sound plays at 0ms (start of the timeline).
narrator: {b}Track 4:{/b} Lavender's expression changes to "happy" at 500ms (just before she stops).
narrator: {b}Track 5:{/b} Camera zooms in slightly over 600ms for intimacy.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: All five tracks run simultaneously. The result: two people walk toward each other, footsteps play, Lavender smiles as she arrives, and the camera pulls in gently.
Lavender: Build this in the Puppeteer editor to see it animated, then read the exported code. That's the fastest way to learn.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

